---
// D429 — bespoke in-kernel reconstruction.
//
// Signature layout, rebuilt 1:1 from the owned reference screenshot: a single
// document sheet centred on the page, cropped at the fold, ruled by one axis —
// the running-text column's left edge. Masthead and body text hang off that
// axis; a step-smaller note column hangs left of it in the margin, each entry
// on a hanging indent, with the artwork parked at the foot of the margin. The
// mechanic here IS the grid (no interaction in the source), so the stage is
// static — proportions carry the study. De-branded: the source masthead
// becomes the work title, its line-drawing doodle becomes a kernel specimen
// plate, and the sheet's ground drops to paper tones (the entry carries no
// palette, so the colour law holds — only --spot marks where artwork sat).
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CaseStudy from '../../components/CaseStudy.astro';

const entry = await getEntry('works', 'd429');
const w = entry!.data;

// The margin column — a second, subordinate voice. Italic entries echo the
// source's asides; the grid never moves for them.
const notes = [
  { text: 'The note column sets a step smaller and hangs every entry from its first line.' },
  { text: 'Asides and citations drop to italic; the measure and the gutter hold.', em: true },
  { text: 'Continuation lines indent, so the margin reads as a ledger of first words.' },
  { text: 'The figure parks at the foot of the margin and never crosses the gutter.' },
];
---

<Base title={`${w.title} — *blue red + purple/`}>
  <CaseStudy work={w} stageLabel="Reconstruction — static, two-column document grid">
    <Fragment slot="narrative">
      <section class="prose">
        <h2>The signature</h2>
        <p>
          The page is a sheet, not a screen: one document block centred against the ground and
          cropped mid-sentence at the fold. Everything obeys a single axis — the masthead and the
          running serif text share one left edge, while the marginal notes hang to its left, set
          smaller, each entry indenting its own continuation lines. A deep band of nothing between
          masthead and text does the pacing; the drawing sits in the margin like a footnote.
        </p>
      </section>
    </Fragment>

    <div class="site">
      <div class="sheet">
        <div class="sheet__grid">
          <header class="masthead">
            <p class="masthead__title ttu">{w.title} Study Doc</p>
            <p class="masthead__sub ttu">Margin note · Running text</p>
          </header>

          <aside class="margin">
            <ul class="margin__notes">
              {notes.map((n) => <li class:list={{ em: n.em }}>{n.text}</li>)}
            </ul>
            <figure class="margin__plate">
              <span class="font-mono">fig. 01</span>
            </figure>
          </aside>

          <div class="body">
            <p>
              <em>One axis rules the sheet,</em> and it belongs to this column. The masthead
              hangs from it, the text hangs from it, and nothing else on the page is allowed an
              opinion about where the left edge sits. What looks like a wide inner margin is the
              cost of that rule — and the page pays it without flinching.
            </p>
            <p>
              The notes opposite are the second voice. They comment, cite, and digress at a
              smaller size, <em>never interrupting the measure of this column,</em> because the
              gutter between the two is a wall, not a suggestion. Each note announces itself with
              a flush first line and tucks the rest behind it.
            </p>
            <p>
              Below the fold the text simply keeps going. The sheet is cropped, not concluded —
              the document grid promises continuity, and the crop is proof that the system runs
              deeper than the viewport. A reader scrolls a document here, not a website, and the
              grid never once breaks character to remind them otherwise.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <section class="prose">
        <h2>Study notes</h2>
        <p>
          The spacing works because the hierarchy is spatial before it is typographic: primary
          text owns the axis, secondary text hangs off it, and rank is legible from position
          alone before a single size difference registers. The oversized band under the masthead
          is the one extravagance, and it is load-bearing — it separates the document's name from
          the document's body the way a title page separates itself from chapter one. Rebuilding
          it meant transcribing three measures — note column, gutter, text column — and letting
          every other decision fall out of them.
        </p>
      </section>
    </Fragment>
  </CaseStudy>
</Base>

<style>
  /* The rebuilt room. Proportions transcribed from the reference: sheet at
     60% of the canvas, offset from the top, running off the bottom edge —
     the crop is part of the layout. Type sizes in cqw so the transcription
     holds at any stage width. */
  .site {
    container-type: inline-size;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 10;
    background-color: var(--paper);
    color: var(--ink);
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .sheet {
    width: 60.3%;
    min-height: 100%;
    margin: 0 auto;
    translate: 0 2.7cqw; /* 4.3% of the canvas height, width-relative */
    background-color: var(--paper-1, var(--paper));
    border: 1px solid var(--line, var(--ink-2));
    border-bottom: none;
    padding: 5.3cqw 2.2cqw 0 2.5cqw;
  }

  /* One grid carries the whole sheet: notes 27 / gutter 5.4 / text 60 (of the
     sheet width). The masthead spans only the text column — the axis rule. */
  .sheet__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 27fr 60fr;
    column-gap: 5.4%;
    align-items: start;
  }

  .masthead {
    grid-column: 2;
    margin-bottom: 10cqw; /* the deep band between name and body */
  }
  .masthead__title {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: var(--wght-display, 700);
    font-size: 3cqw;
    line-height: 1.05;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-display, -0.02em);
  }
  .masthead__sub {
    margin: 0.9em 0 0;
    font-size: 1.5cqw;
    font-weight: 400;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    color: var(--ink-1, var(--ink));
  }

  .margin {
    grid-column: 1;
  }
  .margin__notes {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    font-size: 0.95cqw;
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--ink-1, var(--ink));
  }
  /* Hanging indent: first line flush, continuation lines tucked behind it. */
  .margin__notes li {
    padding-left: 2.1em;
    text-indent: -2.1em;
  }
  .margin__notes li.em {
    font-style: italic;
  }

  /* Where the source parks its line drawing: a de-branded specimen plate at
     the foot of the margin. The spot ink marks "artwork carries colour". */
  .margin__plate {
    margin: 3.8cqw 0 0;
    aspect-ratio: 5 / 6;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    background-color: var(--paper);
    border: 1px solid var(--line, var(--ink-2));
  }
  .margin__plate span {
    font-size: 1.1cqw;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--spot, var(--ink));
  }

  .body {
    grid-column: 2;
    font-size: 1.2cqw;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  .body p {
    margin: 0;
  }
  /* Source rhythm: italic lead-ins, indented follow-on paragraphs, and a
     half-line of air only where a new thought starts. */
  .body p + p {
    margin-top: 0.55em;
    text-indent: 1.8em;
  }
</style>
